1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:02,320
Personally, I felt welcomed.

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:06,200
When I arrived in this village, there were so many moments when I was able to meet people,

3
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:08,500
whether around the school or around the City Stade,

4
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:13,080
where all generations come together during events and activities,

5
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:14,100
and also through local associations.

6
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:18,260
And I think that for people who may feel a bit marginal, especially artists,

7
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:20,500
they find their place in this environment.

8
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:23,420
I completely agree with that analysis.

9
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:26,220
I’m also a newcomer myself, because in the end, I’ve only been here for fourteen years.

10
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:29,300
First of all, I was captivated by the village as soon as I arrived.

11
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:31,220
It was the first time that had ever happened to me.

12
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:32,960
I crossed the Aveyron bridge there.

13
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:35,160
I arrived beneath these magnificent ramparts.

14
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:36,040
I walked through the streets.

15
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:37,520
I was immediately charmed.

16
00:00:37,580 --> 00:00:38,900
I said to myself, “This is where I would like to live.”

17
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:40,380
And I made friends.

18
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:42,060
You integrate very quickly into Montricoux,

19
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:45,280
where people talk to one another and everyone knows each other.

20
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:47,380
There is a lively town center, there is a market that thrives,

21
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:50,320
and the associations are indeed very dynamic.

22
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:53,440
There is a strong identity here,

23
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:56,200
something people truly want to preserve:

24
00:00:57,760 --> 00:00:59,000
living together as a community.

25
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:01,720
As you said, everyone knows each other,

26
00:01:01,770 --> 00:01:04,260
both longtime residents and newcomers,

27
00:01:05,260 --> 00:01:09,780
as well as younger and older generations alike.

28
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:12,300
And so, this journey is also a reflection of both the artists of the past

29
00:01:12,690 --> 00:01:16,960
and those who are here today. It simply reflects what can be experienced every day in this village.

30
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:20,220
And the fact that there is such a great diversity of artists,

31
00:01:20,290 --> 00:01:23,440
for me, is also connected to the geography of Montricoux.

32
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:29,360
It is a village that, to me, exists between two worlds.

33
00:01:29,860 --> 00:01:32,160
It is both oriented toward urban life

34
00:01:33,340 --> 00:01:34,060
looking toward

35
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:36,160
Nègrepelisse, Montauban, and Caussade

36
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:37,880
and at the same time

37
00:01:38,290 --> 00:01:39,940
oriented toward rural life,

38
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:42,140
toward the Aveyron Gorges. In fact, this means

39
00:01:42,190 --> 00:01:43,860
two things:

40
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:46,100
when one goes toward Montauban and the city,

41
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:48,100
it is more associated with artistic

42
00:01:48,180 --> 00:01:50,180
and cultural development. And at the same time,

43
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:52,220
there are artists living here

44
00:01:52,380 --> 00:01:54,200
in a rural environment, and this too

45
00:01:54,290 --> 00:01:56,020
is the result of an artistic reflection:

46
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:57,379
a return to something

47
00:01:57,960 --> 00:01:58,940
slower.

48
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:01,380
I think that artists today,

49
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:03,360
just like those of yesterday,

50
00:02:03,540 --> 00:02:05,420
walk through the forests,

51
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:06,920
sit by the riverbanks.

52
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:09,200
A musician, I think,

53
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:11,140
when you are by a river, that is music in itself.

54
00:02:11,260 --> 00:02:13,400
All these sounds are inspiring,

55
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:14,780
whatever the era.

56
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:17,100
And nature itself is constantly changing.

57
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:19,220
The light here is incredibly powerful.

58
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:21,100
And when we are in a place like this,

59
00:02:21,580 --> 00:02:25,620
where we have access to natural environments,

60
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:28,620
it sharpens the way we look at the world.

61
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:30,340
And I think that is true in every era.

62
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:35,240
Indeed, when we look at Marcel-Lenoir, he painted many landscapes.

63
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:42,500
And Louis Cazottes was somewhat in the same spirit when he said: “I have traveled all over the world, but the most beautiful place on Earth is Montricoux.”

64
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:44,320
So yes, I think that is part of it.

65
00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:52,340
The connection between the artistic world, people’s passion for art, but also for the place where they live,

66
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:55,160
and the passion they share with one another,

67
00:02:56,060 --> 00:02:59,660
creates a kind of protective cocoon around us.

68
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:03,980
So thank you to André Lacan, who was one of the first people

69
00:03:03,990 --> 00:03:06,860
with whom I had conversations about this subject. He was deeply passionate.

70
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:10,820
And he also cared deeply about the inhabitants,

71
00:03:10,830 --> 00:03:14,980
because for him, the village could not exist without the soul of the village, which is its people.

72
00:03:15,540 --> 00:03:19,420
He truly was an extraordinary man in the village, and he is greatly missed.

73
00:03:23,940 --> 00:03:25,300
It’s difficult for me to speak about him, because he was a friend.

74
00:03:26,820 --> 00:03:31,960
We shared many things together, and we had many common views about respect and the way people should live together.

75
00:03:32,480 --> 00:03:38,960
I think maintaining that connection is very important, and I believe artists are able to offer us that.

76
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:47,620
So Montricoux means “rugged hill,” “harsh hill,” because we are here upon a hill.

77
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:52,640
And perhaps in earlier times, it was difficult to climb it or even to reach it.

78
00:03:53,180 --> 00:03:55,200
A harsh hill.

79
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:57,180
Every time I walk through the village,

80
00:03:58,330 --> 00:03:58,940
everything speaks to me:

81
00:03:59,330 --> 00:04:00,340
the walls, the streets,

82
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:02,360
everything!
